      "math_academy_id": "q-98016",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/0b2dd71389af5f66bc7931819038922728c3533f89d0de9234514ee24a9d33a5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)-3?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)-3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/e2b35429545d9e5c8b4e9217908b4c3755790425ff87cfb699da7aae47f6dc6b.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result down by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(-x)-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/1b81c0a3c72fa80d35d5dccb462afeb2ba3f1b05c10f009f300fdfd170ed9e76.png)",
